### Impact and severity

- Affected: agents with Tool Search in `tools` mode (the default) whose model calls a deferred tool by its own name.
- Severity: the call fails with no recovery hint; a model that retries ends the turn.
- Frequency: every direct call to a deferred name in `tools` mode (deterministic in the repro).
- Consequence: the tool is not used even though it is authorized, and the reply says nothing useful about why.

### Additional information

- First noticed with `anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5` on 2026.10.7, which called `ask_user` and `web_search` directly in `tools` mode. The stub reproduces the same runtime path deterministically.
- Code path: `resolveDeferredTool` (`packages/agent-core/src/agent-loop.ts`) is only wired in `directory` mode (`src/agents/embedded-agent-runner/run/attempt-session-prepare.ts`), so `tools` mode falls through to `Tool ${name} not found`.
- Earlier report of this path: #184068 (a structured direct `skills_read` call got `Tool skills_read not found`). #184133 fixed it for skills by changing the skills prompt to name the dispatcher; other deferred tools still get the bare not-found.
- Related, different paths: #183085 / #183165 (calls printed as text), #161022 (the reverse case, `dispatch_action` on a direct-only tool), and the Code Mode report #179823 (closed as a duplicate of #161072).

Reproduction below uses mocked dispatch and no real databases.

## Affected build and observation

OpenClaw 2026.11.2 (b556fdf), Node 26.13.1, macOS. Nightly memory promotion reported scheduler success but the actual completion log showed 11 failed workspaces out of 12. Diagnostic evidence showed the worker's 64-client capacity reached while only two stores remained active. This is consistent with leaked reservations; the synchronous-failure paths below are demonstrated defects, but the observation alone does not establish they explain every live leaked reservation.

## Defect

The broker registers a client before calculating request bytes and calling `inputAdmission.open(...)`. A synchronous exception during those operations occurs before the returned promise's `.catch(...)` cleanup is attached, leaving the client registered.

Input admission increments its retained-byte budget before `runWithSqliteDatabaseAdmissionTurn(...)` returns. A synchronous exception occurs before `.finally(release)` is attached, leaving bytes retained.

## Proposed fix

Preserve synchronous admission ordering. Wrap each synchronous call boundary in `try/catch`; on failure delete the broker client or call the admission release function, respectively, then return a rejected promise. Retain the existing asynchronous cleanup paths. Apply to the source implementations, not only generated distribution bundles.

## Regression coverage

The included `regression.mjs` extracts actual classes from a distribution bundle and checks:

1. Request-byte calculation throws synchronously: client reservation released.
2. Admission throws synchronously: retained bytes and open tracking released.
3. Broker propagation of admission failure: client and retained bytes released.
4. Asynchronous dispatch failure: all reservations released.
5. Successful admission: client retained until close; input bytes released.
6. Capacity rejection: no additional reservation added.

The original build fails three of the six checks. The locally repaired copies pass all six checks in each of six generated bundles (36 checks). The post-restart promotion processed 12 workspaces with zero failures and zero degraded results; no eligible candidates were applied, and one narrative remained pending. A clean promotion run verifies recovery but not long-term absence of every possible leak.

## Expanded bundle inventory

An upgrade-safeguard scan also found three generated `.js` copies missed by the original `.mjs`-only scan. All three reproduced the same 3/6 baseline failures and passed 6/6 after the same cleanup change. The full reviewed inventory is nine bundles, with 54 behavioral checks.

## Maintainer request

Add equivalent source-level regression tests and the synchronous cleanup fix to the SQLite broker and input-admission implementations. Review other synchronous admission paths for similar promise-cleanup gaps. Consider making promotion's scheduler outcome reflect per-workspace failures.

## Reproduction harness

Save the following as `regression.mjs`, then run `node regression.mjs /path/to/affected/bundle.mjs`. This extracts the two actual classes, mocks dispatch, and does not open a database. Generated names vary by build. Confirm the bundle contains `SqliteWorkerInputAdmission` and `SqliteWorkerBroker`. Expected: 6/6 pass; affected original build: 3/6 pass.

```js
import fs from 'node:fs';
import vm from 'node:vm';
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';

const file = process.argv[2];
if (!file) throw new Error('Usage: node regression.mjs /path/to/openclaw/dist/sqlite-worker-store-BUILD.mjs');
const source = fs.readFileSync(file, 'utf8');
function extract(name) {
  const match = new RegExp(`^(\\t*)(?:var )?${name} = class \\{`, 'm').exec(source);
  assert(match, `missing ${name}`);
  const start = match.index;
  const tail = source.slice(start);
  const end = new RegExp(`^${match[1]}\\};`, 'm').exec(tail);
  assert(end, `missing end ${name}`);
  // Unused openAdmitted implementation references import.meta; VM is a script.
  return ('var ' + tail.slice(match[1].length, end.index + end[0].length).replace(/^var /,'')).replaceAll('import.meta.resolve', 'unusedImportMetaResolve');
}
let mode = 'success';
const context = vm.createContext({
  SqliteWorkerError: Error,
  MAX_STORES: 64,
  validateSqliteWorkerDatabaseLocator() {},
  toErrorObject: e => e,
  captureSqliteWorkerOpen: options => ({...options, input: new Uint8Array(7)}),
  sqliteWorkerRequestBytes() { if(mode === 'bytes-sync') throw Error('bytes-sync'); return 7; },
  runWithSqliteDatabaseAdmissionTurn(paths, operation) {
    if(mode === 'admission-sync') throw Error('admission-sync');
    return operation();
  },
  runQueuedStoreWrite({fn}) { return fn(); },
});
vm.runInContext(`${extract('SqliteWorkerInputAdmission')}\n${extract('SqliteWorkerBroker')}\nglobalThis.Classes = {SqliteWorkerInputAdmission,SqliteWorkerBroker};`, context);
const {SqliteWorkerInputAdmission: Admission, SqliteWorkerBroker: Broker} = context.Classes;
function makeAdmission() {
  return new Admission({maxMessageBytes:100,maxQueuedBytes:1000,queuedBytes:()=>0,isClosing:()=>false});
}
function makeBroker(admission=makeAdmission()) {
  const broker = Object.create(Broker.prototype);
  Object.assign(broker, {clients:new Set(),inputAdmission:admission});
  broker.openAdmitted = async (options, token) => {
    if(mode === 'dispatch-async') throw Error('dispatch-async');
    return {close:async()=>broker.clients.delete(token)};
  };
  return broker;
}
const cases = [
  ['request byte calculation sync throw', async()=> {
    mode='bytes-sync'; const b=makeBroker();
    await assert.rejects(async()=>b.open({databasePath:'/fake'}), /bytes-sync/);
    assert.equal(b.clients.size,0);
  }],
  ['admission sync throw releases bytes', async()=> {
    mode='admission-sync'; const a=makeAdmission();
    await assert.rejects(async()=>a.open(7,'/fake',()=>Promise.resolve()), /admission-sync/);
    assert.equal(a.retainedBytes,0); assert.equal(a.opens.size,0);
  }],
  ['admission sync throw releases broker client', async()=> {
    mode='admission-sync'; const b=makeBroker();
    await assert.rejects(async()=>b.open({databasePath:'/fake'}), /admission-sync/);
    assert.equal(b.clients.size,0); assert.equal(b.inputAdmission.retainedBytes,0);
  }],
  ['async dispatch failure releases all reservations', async()=> {
    mode='dispatch-async'; const b=makeBroker();
    await assert.rejects(async()=>b.open({databasePath:'/fake'}), /dispatch-async/);
    assert.equal(b.clients.size,0); assert.equal(b.inputAdmission.retainedBytes,0);
    assert.equal(b.inputAdmission.opens.size,0);
  }],
  ['successful admission retains client until mock close', async()=> {
    mode='success'; const b=makeBroker(); const s=await b.open({databasePath:'/fake'});
    assert.equal(b.clients.size,1); assert.equal(b.inputAdmission.retainedBytes,0);
    await s.close(); assert.equal(b.clients.size,0);
  }],
  ['capacity rejects without adding reservations', async()=> {
    mode='success'; const b=makeBroker(); for(let i=0;i<64;i++) b.clients.add({});
    await assert.rejects(async()=>b.open({databasePath:'/fake'}), /capacity/);
    assert.equal(b.clients.size,64); assert.equal(b.inputAdmission.retainedBytes,0);
  }],
];
let failures=0;
for(const [name,test] of cases) {try {await test();console.log(`PASS ${name}`);}catch(e){failures++;console.log(`FAIL ${name}: ${e.message}`);}}
console.log(`${cases.length-failures}/${cases.length} passed; actual classes extracted from ${file}`);
process.exitCode=failures?1:0;
```

## Related report

Related but distinct: #162497 concerns capacity exhaustion from legitimate active stores. This report demonstrates reservations retained after synchronous open failures, even when the corresponding store never opens.

